<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Passion of Saints Perpetua and Felicity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">203</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Passion of Saints Perpetua and Felicity is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> account of the martyrdom of a group of Christians in the amphitheatre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in 203. It includes a prison diary attributed to Vibia Perpetua, a young noblewoman, making it one of the earliest surviving texts written by a Christian woman. Her slave Felicity, who gave birth in prison, died with her.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/passion-of-saints-perpetua-and-felicity.jpg" alt="Passion of Saints Perpetua and Felicity" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benedictine abbey of Saint Gallen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Passio_sanctarum_Perpetuae_et_Felicitatis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>A short introduction by the editor (chapters i-ii) is followed by Perpetua's own narrative and visions (iii-ix), the vision of Saturus (xi-xiii) and the editor's account of the deaths (xiv-xxi), which he claims to have witnessed. Perpetua writes in the first person of her father, who pleads with her to recant, her baptism, her move to prison, and the infant she nursed there, whose charge she gave to her mother and brother.</p><p>In her visions she climbs a ladder hung with weapons past a serpent into a garden, sees her dead brother Dinocrates first suffering and then healed, and fights a savage Egyptian, which she takes to mean a battle with the Devil himself. After a hearing before the governor Hilarianus, the martyrs were scourged, set upon by a boar, a bear, a leopard and a wild cow, and finally put to the sword.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The martyrdoms were long blamed on a decree of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> against conversion, but that decree is known only from the unreliable Augustan History, and the deaths in the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> were probably the result of local persecution. The text survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek forms.</p><p>Most scholars accept that Perpetua wrote her section, which would make her one of the very few Christian women writers before the fourth century whose work survives. The editor's identity is uncertain; some have proposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tertullian">Tertullian</a>, and his style suggests a man. Brent Shaw argues that the editor affirms her martyrdom while presenting her actions as unnatural, and the vision of Saturus may itself be the editor's work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Some scholars have read a Montanist theology in its stress on new prophecies and visions, though nothing in it is explicitly Montanist, and Timothy David Barnes withdrew his own earlier case in 1982. The healing of Dinocrates raised the question of unbaptised children: Augustine answered a monk named Renatus, who used it to argue that they could reach paradise.</p><p>The rose Félicité et Perpétue was introduced by Robert Jacques in 1828. A translation by Walter H. Shewring in 1929 was printed in a new typeface by Eric Gill, which took the name Perpetua.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Passion of Saints Perpetua and Felicity” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
